    ...text('The README used to hold this plan. When commit 0b0eee8 turned the README into a screenshot showcase on 2026-10-14, the plan had to go somewhere that would not be lost, and the answer was an archive file that keeps what was done and how it was checked. The header says exactly that: the plan was a wish list of features missed from the JetBrains workflow, written while using RustRover 2026.2, then eleven steps and several rounds of field testing.'),
    ...code('markdown', r'docs/HISTORY.md · lines 3-3', r'''
The step-by-step plan this config was built from (my wish list of features missed from the JetBrains workflow, written while using RustRover 2026.2, then eleven steps and several rounds of field testing), kept as an archive: what was done, how it was verified and what was not. The current state is described in the [README](../README.md) and [DETAILS.md](DETAILS.md); the rounds of fixes are in the [CHANGELOG](../CHANGELOG.md).'''),
    ...text('The wish list itself has a method that shows in the second paragraph. It was researched on 2026-10-08 in 15 dimensions, each adversarially verified, items from unverified research are marked as such, and steps are ordered by value, each verifiable by a health check, a headless smoke run or a real crate. The editor already did well on diagnostics latency, startup, raw rust-analyzer access and scriptability, so the list is mostly tooling that was installed but inert or unbound.'),
    ...sec('the eleven steps in order'),
    ...bullet('1. Reality check', 'the first finding is that the clippy tier was dead: the local patched bacon-ls and its cmd override pointed at nothing. Fixed by removing the override once crisidev/bacon-ls#139 shipped in 0.31.0. mold and a cargo config were also missing and were installed.'),
    ...bullet('2. Extras and packages', 'the LazyVim extras dap.core, test.core, overseer, toml, yaml, json, markdown and git were enabled and codelens turned on; the npm dependency of Mason packages is noted.'),
    ...bullet('3. rust-analyzer command keymaps', 'the previously unbound rustaceanvim commands got buffer-local keys under leader r (refactor, inspect, explain error, symbols, server), and the leader cR rename was restored.'),
    ...bullet('4. rust-analyzer settings pass', 'keys checked against the server’s own config schema: references that skip imports and tests, an eight-thread budget for a 16-core machine, term search, extra postfix snippets.'),
    ...bullet('5. Safety net', 'Local History as git refs, the undotree key, and the 99 guard with a bubblewrap sandbox. This is the longest step and is described below.'),
    ...bullet('6. Git depth', 'diffview, mergiraf as a merge driver for Rust files, zdiff3 and rerere in the global git config, lazygit with delta as pager, and per-repo opt-ins that were deliberately not applied automatically.'),
    ...bullet('7. Navigation and orientation', 'sticky scope, breadcrumbs, a scrollbar with diagnostics and hunks, and go to super method.'),
    ...bullet('8. Context switcher', ':RustContext, :RustTargets and :RustNew, born because LazyVim sets allFeatures while bacon-ls used a hard-coded all-features, so mutually exclusive features could not be tested.'),
    ...bullet('9. Supply chain, coverage, profiling', 'cargo-deny and machete as lint sources, llvm-cov coverage in the gutter, :RustTools for timings and bloat.'),
    ...bullet('10. Domain-specific', 'HTTP files with kulala, :RustRoutes, SQL injected into sqlx strings, wasm, wgsl-analyzer and a note on embedded.'),
    ...bullet('11. Optional', 'the Claude Code bridge, a statusline component, a right-click Rust menu, Neovide settings, a colour-blind diagnostic palette, :checkhealth xynovim and CI.'),
    ...sec('step 5 in detail: the sandbox as it was designed'),
    ...text('Step 5 carries the most engineering, and the file keeps the threat model next to the design. Local History comes first: every save writes the whole worktree as a commit on a private ref with a private index, so deleted files and reset --hard are recoverable. The undotree key is a one-liner.'),
    ...code('markdown', r'docs/HISTORY.md · lines 47-47', r'''
- **Undotree**: `<leader>uu` opens Neovim 0.12's built-in `nvim.undotree`.'''),
    ...text('Then the 99 guard. A wrapper around the Claude provider takes a forced snapshot, pauses auto-save, runs the agent inside bubblewrap and restores afterwards. The sub-bullets list the layers: namespaces unshared (user, pid, ipc, uts, cgroup) with capabilities dropped; a read-only filesystem except the project and the agent’s own state; the .git directory and the agent’s settings and hooks locked read-only; credential directories hidden; environment names scrubbed by pattern. A second hardening pass came from an adversarial re-review on 2026-10-12, a third from a field-test batch.'),
    ...text('The last sub-bullet is a model of honest security writing. It does not claim safety. It states the sandbox is a denylist on a read-only view of the filesystem with the network open, and lists the residual risks: the OAuth credentials file must stay readable and writable so an agent can read and exfiltrate that token, anything unlisted under home is readable, loopback services and abstract sockets are reachable, cargo’s registry sources are writable, and secret names the pattern does not know pass through. The conclusion is the phrase now used in the README.'),
    ...code('markdown', r'docs/HISTORY.md · lines 57-57', r'''
  - It is still a denylist on a read-only view of the filesystem with the network open. Residual risks: `~/.claude/.credentials.json` must stay readable and writable (OAuth refresh), so an agent can read and exfiltrate that token; everything unlisted under `$HOME` is readable; loopback services and abstract sockets are reachable; cargo's `registry/src` and `git` are writable (your own cargo may later build planted code); nested `.git` deeper than 2 levels is not locked; files the project lacks (`.envrc`, `.vscode/tasks.json`, `rust-toolchain.toml`, `bacon.toml`) can be created by the agent (only `.cargo` and `.claude` are masked when absent), and secret names the pattern does not know (`STRIPE_KEY` is covered by its prefix, an arbitrary `FOO_KEY` is not) pass through; the project's source and `build.rs` are the agent's by design. Treat it as a seatbelt, not a jail. Opt out with `vim.g.xynovim_99_sandbox = false`.'''),
    ...text('That reasoning is why later work did not stop at the sandbox. The sandbox was made fail closed on 2026-10-16 (commit 440d781), and the provider picker was restricted to the guarded provider. KNOWN_ISSUES.md still lists the open items, such as the writable ~/.claude state directories.'),
    ...sec('the deliberate omissions'),
    ...text('Two sections near the end say what was not done and why, and they are as useful as the done lists. Optional or revisit lists things kept in reserve: a memory cap around rust-analyzer, snapper snapshots of home on btrfs, direnv, cargo-hack feature powersets, rr reverse debugging. Not worth chasing lists things judged to have too little value: type hierarchy over LSP (unsupported), a tabbed search-everything, a full Cargo tool window (a cargo metadata picker reaches about 80 percent), a macro_rules! tester. Individual steps also carry their refusals.'),
    ...code('markdown', r'docs/HISTORY.md · lines 62-62', r'''
- Deliberately not done: gating auto-save on ERROR diagnostics (it would stall `didSave` and bacon-ls); snapper on btrfs `/home` as an extra OS-level layer is optional and not configured.'''),
    ...code('markdown', r'docs/HISTORY.md · lines 118-118', r'''
- **Containers**: not implemented, by design. Recipe: run the whole editor inside `distrobox enter <box>` (shared home, nothing in this config changes) or a devcontainer with nvim installed and this repo cloned to `~/.config/nvim`; `nvim-dev-container` was not added.'''),
    ...text('The containers line is a good example. The step does not build a feature; it gives a recipe (run the editor inside a distrobox or a devcontainer) and records that nvim-dev-container was not added.'),
